WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.502
-Our next guest
is a prolific writer,

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:04.236
legendary filmmaker
and Academy Award-nominated

00:00:04.236 --> 00:00:07.340
director of more than
70 features and documentaries.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.442
His memoir, "Every Man
for Himself and God Against All"

00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:11.811
is on sale now.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:14.547
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Werner Herzog.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:15.982
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:31.163 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.163 --> 00:00:32.499
Welcome back, Werner.

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:34.634
-Thank you.
I like to be back here.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:35.902
-It is wonderful
to have you here.

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:38.938
I'm so enjoying
reading your memoir.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.173
But when you write a memoir

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:42.775
and I have a question
uniquely for you,

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:46.446
what is Werner Herzog's
relationship with the truth?

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:50.616
-Well, when you go
into the memoirs,

00:00:50.616 --> 00:00:53.653
you have to verify
what's -- what happened.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:56.255
So, and some people
are dazed and confused

00:00:56.255 --> 00:01:01.928
because I live such a wild life
as if I had lived five lives.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:05.465
But I sent the manuscript
to my brothers

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:07.867
who are very critical,
and they said,

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:11.571
"Yeah, okay, go ahead."
And then people doubt, "Yeah,

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.339
did he move a ship
over a mountain?"

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.074
Yes, I did. It's documented.

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:16.709
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.445
Did he hypnotize
an entire cast?

00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:24.884
Yes, I did. It's all
in a way verifiable.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:30.290
For example, there was doubt --
I staged an opera once in Italy

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.959
and I wanted to have
some stage work

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:36.796
falling from high up
and through the stage floor.

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:39.899
And everybody is wondering
what the hell happened here.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:43.202
And and the theater
wouldn't give me a stunt man.

00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:47.040
So I tested the stunt myself
from something like...

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.308
[ Audience gasps ]

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:52.244
Something like 45 feet down
into an air cushion.

00:01:52.244 --> 00:01:55.481
And so people say I made it up.
No, I didn't.

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:57.149
There are a series of photos.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:02:00.286
I can show them to you
or whatever. I was --

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:03.490
I was shot during an interview.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.659
It sounds like
"Yes, he made it up."

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.561
No, it's caught on tape.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.596
It was a BBC interview.

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:14.400
So you better be careful
when you do something like that.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.403
If you write poetry,
you just invent.

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:20.907
If you write fiction,
you invent completely.

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:25.144
But here, of course, what is an
inventive element is the style,

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:27.213
the way I'm telling the story.

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:31.316
The wild sort of content
needs a certain style.

00:02:31.316 --> 00:02:33.886
-And there is, of course,
a lot of documentary evidence

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:35.855
about the things
you've done either on film

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:37.223
or just in photographs.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.659
You chose the photo
for the cover of this book.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:41.594
This is you on the edge
of a volcano.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:44.030
-Right.
And I had a protective suit.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.698 line:0%
-Yes. But I noticed you took off

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.467 line:0%
this protective part
for the picture.

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:51.203 line:0%
-Yeah, because it was
at the end of it.

00:02:51.203 --> 00:02:56.809
I was with a volcanologist,
and the volcano was spitting out

00:02:56.809 --> 00:03:01.614
slabs of glowing
hot sort of lava,

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:04.117
and it came raining down.
You'd better look up.

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:06.285
Where does it come?
You don't duck.

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:10.123
You have to look and step aside.
So, and we were too close.

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:13.792
And all of a sudden,
some unexpected burst

00:03:13.792 --> 00:03:15.294
and it came raining down.

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:20.332
And some of these slabs
or blobs,

00:03:20.332 --> 00:03:23.902
thousand degrees Celsius,
they come raining down.

00:03:23.902 --> 00:03:27.406
So you better run.
You get out of there.

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:29.042
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:32.444
-And I don't mind
to be a coward in such a moment.

00:03:32.444 --> 00:03:34.613
-But it did seem in that moment
you also said,

00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:35.782
"Should we get a picture?"

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:39.986
[ Laughter ]

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:43.622
-I don't recall, but no,
I never asked for a picture.

00:03:43.622 --> 00:03:45.758
I'm not so important.

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:48.760
The film is important
or the writing is important.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.530
But I had taken off my helmet
because I was sweating

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.667
and I had soot
in my face somehow.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.102
And actually this photo
was done

00:03:57.102 --> 00:04:00.105
by the volcanologist,
Clive Oppenheimer.

00:04:00.105 --> 00:04:03.008
He had his photo camera
and he said, "Stand still.

00:04:03.008 --> 00:04:07.112
I take a few photos."
And I kind of like the photos

00:04:07.112 --> 00:04:12.919
because my life has been on foot
digging in the jungle

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:17.389
and moving a ship
and doing things like that.

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:19.892
-You talk about how -- and this
is fascinating to me --

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.594
you can see a whole film before
you start writing it sometimes.

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:25.797
And you talk about
writing "Aguirre, Wrath of God,"

00:04:25.797 --> 00:04:27.400
which is this movie --

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.268
It's hard to imagine
conceiving this whole story

00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:30.402
and sitting down to write it.

00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:32.238
But when I pictured
where you wrote it,

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.606
I would never have guessed
it was

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:35.574
where you said
you wrote it in the book.

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:38.711
-Well, it was written
in two and a half days.

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:42.949
I saw an entire film
and I was on a bus

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:45.684
with my soccer team
and we went to Italy.

00:04:45.684 --> 00:04:50.356
We had two huge barrels
of Bavarian beer for our hosts.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.359
We tried to make them drunk
and win the game.

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:54.794
So but what happens,

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:58.797
our team started to open
a bottle -- a whole barrel,

00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:00.999
and they were drunk
within two hours.

00:05:00.999 --> 00:05:04.937
And I was sitting at that time
a little typewriter on my knees

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:06.806
and writing the screenplay,

00:05:06.806 --> 00:05:09.308
and even my goalie
leans over me and he says,

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:12.044
"What are you doing?"
Then vomits over my typewriter.

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:14.046
[ Laughter ]
So what can you do?

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:16.649
I threw out a few pages
from the window

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:19.318
and I forgot what was on it.
I forgot.

00:05:19.318 --> 00:05:23.523
So I had to type on
and I wrote the screenplay.

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:28.327
I don't need a quiet island
where I'm all alone

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:30.162
and things are serene.

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:33.833
I can write
on a playground for children

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:37.002
who are throwing toys
at each other and scream

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:39.204
and the dog is barking at me.

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:41.808
It doesn't matter.
I can write any place.

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:44.277
-You wrote something
very funny in the book

00:05:44.277 --> 00:05:46.579
that I would really
like you to do.

00:05:46.579 --> 00:05:49.849
You wrote -- You were talking
about seeing the auctioneers

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:52.017
at a livestock auction,
and you wrote,

00:05:52.017 --> 00:05:55.120
"I always wanted to direct a
'Hamlet' and have all the parts

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.857
played by ex-champion
livestock auctioneers."

00:05:57.857 --> 00:05:59.158
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:01.994
"I wanted the performance
to come in at under 14 minutes."

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:03.595
[ Laughter ]
-Yes.

00:06:03.595 --> 00:06:07.166
Well, still -- still
unfinished project.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:08.434
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:12.538
And, you know, you know,
these livestock auctioneers,

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.607
they speak with a speed
that is unimaginable.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:20.046
For me, the last poetry
of capitalism, so to speak,

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:22.381
and I really want --
anyone knows

00:06:22.381 --> 00:06:24.817
basically what's happening
in "Hamlet."

00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:27.686
And I want to bring it down
under 14 minutes.

00:06:27.686 --> 00:06:29.355
-Yeah.
-Unfinished business.

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:30.555
-There you go.
Well, you have a lot.

00:06:30.555 --> 00:06:32.558
And I would like to --

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.827
There's a film you did
that last time you left,

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:37.095
I so regretted -- and I'm a fan
of so many of your films.

00:06:37.095 --> 00:06:39.898
But "Bad Lieutenant:
Port of Call New Orleans,"

00:06:39.898 --> 00:06:41.167
you mentioned
in the book as well.

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:45.103
It is again a career
of fine performances.

00:06:45.103 --> 00:06:47.439
It might be my favorite
Nicolas Cage performance.

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:49.441
And you concur.

00:06:49.441 --> 00:06:54.647
-And Nicolas Cage
had been interviewed about it.

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:56.716
"What is your favorite
performance?"

00:06:56.716 --> 00:07:00.653
And he says, "There's one,"
"Bad Lieutenant" with me

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:02.221
and then there's nothing
and nothing

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:06.659
and then comes the performance
that won him the Academy Award.

00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:08.294
I think "Leaving Las Vegas."
-"Leaving Las Vegas." Yes.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:11.397
-Yes. And it was so wonderful
to work with him,

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:15.334
such a -- such a great,
great actor.

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:17.936
And I loved every minute
with him.

00:07:17.936 --> 00:07:19.472
And it was wonderful.

00:07:19.472 --> 00:07:21.206
-Well, it shows up
on the screen.

00:07:21.206 --> 00:07:22.575
It is a fantastic film.
-It shows.

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:24.510
-Yes. It's a fantastic book.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.712
And I know you're
as prolific a writer.

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:28.080
You've already
written another book,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.049
which means you get to come
back here again soon.

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:31.950
-Okay. Thank you.
-Thank you very much.

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:33.719
Werner Herzog, everybody.

00:07:33.719 --> 00:07:37.256
"Every Man for Himself and God
Against All" available

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:38.391
wherever you buy your books.

